#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723221 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} y=y^{2} + 2$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} y - y^{2}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=2$, and $f(1,1)=2$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=6 x y\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=3 x^{2} - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(1,1)&=6 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\ &=6\\\\ f_y(1,1)&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right)\\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1)=\\mathbf i-6\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $-\\mathbf i+6\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\mathbf t(1,1)&=\\left(6\\right)\\left(1\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(1\\right)\\left(6\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723237 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. To give area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. We differentiate with respect to the indicated variable, holding the other variable constant.\n\nFor the first field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^y-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=e^y,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=e^y-(e^y-1)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo select the first field. The term $y^2$ in $Q$ is constant with respect to $x$.\n\nFor the second field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=\\sin x,\\\\\nQ_x&=1-\\sin x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=1-2\\sin x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$, so do not select this field. Its curl equals $1$ when $x=0$, but equality at a few points does not meet the requirement for every region.\n\nFor the third field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=-\\frac14,\\\\\nQ_x&=\\frac34,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\frac34-\\left(-\\frac14\\right)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe terms $\\cos(x^2)$ in $P$ and $y^3$ in $Q$ disappear in these partial derivatives. Select the third field.\n\nTherefore, select the **first and third fields**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723245 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following pairs of functions make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nI. $P(x,y)=y+x^2$ and $Q(x,y)=x+y^2$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=-y+x^2$ and $Q(x,y)=y^2$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3x+y^2$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. To give area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. We differentiate with respect to the indicated variable, holding the other variable constant.\n\n- **I:** $P_y=1$ and $Q_x=1$, so $Q_x-P_y=1-1=0$. This pair does not give area.\n- **II:** $P_y=-1$ and $Q_x=0$, so $Q_x-P_y=0-(-1)=1$. This pair gives area.\n- **III:** $P_y=2$ and $Q_x=3$, so $Q_x-P_y=3-2=1$. This pair also gives area.\n\nThe $x^2$ terms in $P$ and the $y^2$ terms in $Q$ disappear in the required partial derivatives. Therefore, the answer is **II and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723247 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. To give area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. We differentiate with respect to the indicated variable, holding the other variable constant.\n\nFor the first field, use both the product and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy)-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy),\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two variable terms cancel, leaving $1$. Select this field.\n\nFor the second field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}-\\frac12,\\\\\nQ_x&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+\\frac12,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\frac12-\\left(-\\frac12\\right)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, both variable terms cancel. Select this field.\n\nFor the third field,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy)-1,\\\\\nQ_x&=\\sin(xy)+xy\\cos(xy)+2x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=1+2x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe trigonometric terms cancel, but $2x$ remains. This curl is not identically $1$; for example, it is $3$ at $x=1$. Do not select this field. Agreement on the line $x=0$ is insufficient for every region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nTherefore, select the **first and second fields**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723270 :question/problem "![Exponentially transformed circle enclosing D](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle e^{\\cos t},e^{\\sin t}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\nUsing $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$, the area can be written as\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\n=\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nWhich expression is the pointwise integrand obtained from this $P$, $Q$ and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign appropriate to the orientation shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a counterclockwise boundary. This also follows from the parametrization: at $t=0$ it starts at $(e,1)$ and moves upward. The map $(u,v)\\mapsto(e^u,e^v)$ preserves the orientation and simplicity of the unit circle.\n\nThe chosen field has scalar curl\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\nGreen's theorem therefore gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(-y)\\,\\mathrm dx.\n$$\nDifferentiate $x(t)$ by the chain rule:\n$$\nx^{\\prime}(t)=-\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t}.\n$$\nSubstituting both the coefficient and the differential gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)\\\\\n&=-e^{\\sin t}\\left(-\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence the required setup is\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin t\\,e^{\\cos t+\\sin t}\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nUsing $x\\,\\mathrm dy$ instead would give a different pointwise integrand. That belongs to the other unit-curl field $P=0$, $Q=x$, rather than the field specified here."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723273 :question/problem "![Clockwise power curve enclosing D](images/10/10f4094edb09afc7908bd0cd3df089387c8f5b762ea5ef86da152d1c9d08b5e4.png)\n\nLet $D$ be enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin^5 t,\\cos^5 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\nUsing $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$, the area can be written as\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\n=\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nWhich expression is the pointwise integrand obtained from this $P$, $Q$ and parametrization using Green's theorem, with its sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "The curve is simple and piecewise smooth, with cusps at its axis intercepts. It is traversed clockwise. For the given field,\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1-0=1.\n$$\nBecause the orientation is negative, area is the negative of the circulation:\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\\right)=-\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm dy.\n$$\nThe chain rule gives\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(\\cos^5 t)=-5\\cos^4 t\\sin t.\n$$\nSubstitute into the orientation-adjusted expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=-x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)\\\\\n&=-(\\sin^5 t)(-5\\cos^4 t\\sin t)\\\\\n&=5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}5\\sin^6 t\\cos^4 t\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nOne minus sign comes from clockwise orientation and the other from differentiating $\\cos t$. They cancel. The powers of $\\sin t$ add to $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723274 :question/problem "![Counterclockwise ellipse enclosing D](images/1d/1de9544b8da50f6280920adf2292da517a844b8b4143f58f646da0a01ffad16c.png)\n\nThe positively oriented curve $C$ encloses $D$ and is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\nUse $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Their scalar curl is $1$, so Green's theorem gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm dy=\\int_0^{2\\pi} f(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nHere $x(t)=3\\cos t$ and $y^{\\prime}(t)=2\\cos t$.\n\nWhich expression is the pointwise integrand $f(t)=x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For this counterclockwise boundary, Green's theorem applies with a positive sign because\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1-0=1.\n$$\nIn $\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm dy$, replace $x$ by $x(t)$ and $\\mathrm dy$ by $y^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)&=(3\\cos t)(2\\cos t)\\\\\n&=6\\cos^2 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\cos^2 t\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nThe requested expression is $6\\cos^2 t$. Although $6\\sin^2 t$ has the same integral over this interval, it is not the pointwise product $x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723289 :question/problem "Find $(2f(t)+3g(t))^{\\prime}$, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=- 3 i \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 j \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 k \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ g(t)&=- 2 i - 2 j \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + k \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 5 k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(2f(t)+3g(t))^{\\prime}=2f^{\\prime}(t)+3g^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(t)&=3 i \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 j \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 k \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t)&=2 j \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + k \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2f^{\\prime}(t)&=6 i \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 12 j \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 k \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ 3g^{\\prime}(t)&=6 j \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 k \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(2f(t)+3g(t))^{\\prime}=6 i \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 18 j \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 5 k \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nKeep the displayed inner arguments when applying the scalar derivative rules; any nonunit inner derivative multiplies its component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723301 :question/problem "Find $(-f(t)-g(t)+3h(t))^{\\prime}$, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=\\langle -1,\\,0,\\,-1\\rangle \\\\ g(t)&=\\langle 2 t,\\,0,\\,2 t\\rangle \\\\ h(t)&=\\langle 2 t^{2},\\,0,\\,t^{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(-f(t)-g(t)+3h(t))^{\\prime}=-f^{\\prime}(t)-g^{\\prime}(t)+3h^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 0,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 2,\\,0,\\,2\\rangle \\\\ h^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 4 t,\\,0,\\,2 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1f^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 0,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ -1g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle -2,\\,0,\\,-2\\rangle \\\\ 3h^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 12 t,\\,0,\\,6 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(-f(t)-g(t)+3h(t))^{\\prime}=\\langle 12 t - 2,\\,0,\\,6 t - 2\\rangle.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723302 :question/problem "Find $(3f(t)-3g(t))^{\\prime}$, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=\\langle 12 - 2 t,\\,7 t,\\,3 t - 1\\rangle \\\\ g(t)&=\\langle 2 t^{2} - 2,\\,- t,\\,- 2 t^{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(3f(t)-3g(t))^{\\prime}=3f^{\\prime}(t)-3g^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle -2,\\,7,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 4 t,\\,-1,\\,- 4 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3f^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle -6,\\,21,\\,9\\rangle \\\\ -3g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle - 12 t,\\,3,\\,12 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(3f(t)-3g(t))^{\\prime}=\\langle - 12 t - 6,\\,24,\\,12 t + 9\\rangle.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723303 :question/problem "Find $(-2f(t)-2g(t))^{\\prime}$, where $t>0$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - \\sqrt{t} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ g(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - t \\\\ t^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(-2f(t)-2g(t))^{\\prime}=-2f^{\\prime}(t)-2g^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t^{2}} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 t^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2f^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{4}{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -2g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{2}{t^{2}} \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 8 t^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(-2f(t)-2g(t))^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{4}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ - 8 t^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723310 :question/problem "Find $(-3g(t)+3h(t))^{\\prime}$, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t)&=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,6 t - 6,\\,- t^{2}\\rangle \\\\ h(t)&=\\langle 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t,\\,4\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(-3g(t)+3h(t))^{\\prime}=-3g^{\\prime}(t)+3h^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,6,\\,- 2 t\\rangle \\\\ h^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,1,\\,0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3g^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,-18,\\,6 t\\rangle \\\\ 3h^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,3,\\,0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(-3g(t)+3h(t))^{\\prime}=\\langle 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,-15,\\,6 t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nKeep the displayed inner arguments when applying the scalar derivative rules; any nonunit inner derivative multiplies its component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723324 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is parametrized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\nIt traverses the boundary of $D$ once counterclockwise. Use Green's theorem with $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$ to find the area of $D$.\n\n![The ellipse with intercepts (plus or minus 2, 0) and (0, plus or minus 1), traversed counterclockwise.](images/29/29f6e1c802a936c7f5a2fb8d55ff228dc1504a00ea663710f43d4880d2b4cfca.png)" :question/worked-solution "The chosen field has unit scalar curl:\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\nSince $C$ is positively oriented, Green's theorem gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\iint_D 1\\,dA=-\\oint_C y\\,dx.\n$$\nHere $y(t)=\\sin t$ and $x'(t)=-2\\sin t$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin t(-2\\sin t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1-\\cos 2t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=\\left[t-\\tfrac12\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe area is $2\\pi$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723333 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\sin 2t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq\\pi.\n$$\nThe arrows indicate the direction of increasing $t$.\n\n![A loop to the right of the y-axis, from the origin along its upper branch and back along its lower branch, with clockwise arrows.](images/9f/9f80d82b0d4c4c7e7d8d59ffbbd059fa57d02d254af79b514cdef81a5a81d816.png)" :question/worked-solution "The path starts at $(0,0)$, follows the upper branch to $(1,0)$, and returns along the lower branch. It is clockwise, so it has negative orientation.\n\nChoose $P=-y$ and $Q=0$. Their scalar curl is $1$. Green's theorem with the orientation correction gives\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-\\oint_C(P\\,dx+Q\\,dy)=\\oint_C y\\,dx.\n$$\nSince $x'(t)=\\cos t$ and $y(t)=\\sin 2t$,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=\\int_0^\\pi\\sin 2t\\cos t\\,dt\\\\\n&=2\\int_0^\\pi\\sin t\\cos^2t\\,dt.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSet $u=\\cos t$, so $du=-\\sin t\\,dt$. The limits become $1$ and $-1$. Hence\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-2\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\n=\\frac{2}{3}\\left[u^3\\right]_{-1}^{1}\n=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\nThe area is $\\frac43$ square units. Using $-\\oint_C y\\,dx$ without correcting the clockwise orientation would give the negative of the area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723335 :question/problem "The boundary $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ encloses a region $D$, where\n\n- $C_1$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf r_1(t)=\\langle t^3,-t^4\\rangle$ for $-1\\leq t\\leq1$;\n- $C_2$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf r_2(t)=\\langle-t,-1\\rangle$ for $-1\\leq t\\leq1$.\n\nTraverse $C_1$ and then $C_2$ in the directions of increasing parameter. Use Green's theorem to find the area of $D$.\n\n![The curved upper boundary C1 runs from (-1,-1) through (0,0) to (1,-1). The horizontal lower boundary C2 returns to (-1,-1).](images/d5/d58009320fabb8ea68bbc67a0c62198965cd5297ba287f5e27e8d45d064269eb.png)" :question/worked-solution "The endpoints join: $C_1$ goes from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,-1)$ through $(0,0)$, and $C_2$ returns from $(1,-1)$ to $(-1,-1)$. The boundary is clockwise.\n\nChoose $P=0$ and $Q=x$, whose scalar curl is $1$. Because the orientation is negative,\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-\\oint_C x\\,dy\n=-\\int_{C_1}x\\,dy-\\int_{C_2}x\\,dy.\n$$\nOn $C_1$, $x=t^3$ and $dy=-4t^3\\,dt$. On $C_2$, $y=-1$ is constant, so $dy=0$. Therefore\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)&=-\\int_{-1}^1t^3(-4t^3)\\,dt-\\int_{-1}^1(-t)\\cdot0\\,dt\\\\\n&=4\\int_{-1}^1t^6\\,dt\\\\\n&=4\\left[\\frac{t^7}{7}\\right]_{-1}^1\\\\\n&=4\\left(\\frac17+\\frac17\\right)=\\frac87.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe straight segment contributes zero for this choice of field; it is still needed to close the boundary. The area is $\\frac87$ square units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723346 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=t + 2,\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 t \\\\ 2 \\\\ t^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component.\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=1.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 t^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both contributions and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}2 t \\\\ 2 \\\\ t^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(t + 2\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 t^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $\\mathbf h(t)=u(t)\\mathbf f(t)$. For each component, the two terms are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h_{1}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(2 t\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(2 t + 4\\big)\\\\&=4 t + 4\\\\h_{2}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(2\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(0\\big)\\\\&=2\\\\h_{3}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(t^{3}\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(3 t^{2} \\left(t + 2\\right)\\big)\\\\&=4 t^{3} + 6 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 t + 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 t^{3} + 6 t^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723347 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=- 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t^{3} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} \\\\ - 2 t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume $t>0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component.\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=- \\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t^{2} \\\\ - 4 t \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both contributions and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(- \\frac{2}{t}\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t^{3} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} \\\\ - 2 t\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t^{2} \\\\ - 4 t \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $\\mathbf h(t)=u(t)\\mathbf f(t)$. For each component, the two terms are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h_{1}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(4 t^{2}\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(12 t^{2} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\big)\\\\&=12 t^{2} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 t^{2}\\\\h_{2}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(4 t\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(8 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\big)\\\\&=8 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 t\\\\h_{3}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(4\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\big)\\\\&=4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 t^{2} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 t^{2} \\\\ 8 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 t \\\\ 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723353 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=- t - 1,\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ - 2 t^{2} \\\\ 2 t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component.\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ - 4 t \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both contributions and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(-1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ - 2 t^{2} \\\\ 2 t\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- t - 1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ - 4 t \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $\\mathbf h(t)=u(t)\\mathbf f(t)$. For each component, the two terms are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h_{1}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(2\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(0\\big)\\\\&=2\\\\h_{2}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(2 t^{2}\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- 4 t \\left(- t - 1\\right)\\big)\\\\&=6 t^{2} + 4 t\\\\h_{3}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(- 2 t\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- 2 t - 2\\big)\\\\&=- 4 t - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 t^{2} + 4 t \\\\ - 4 t - 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723365 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=- 2 t - 2,\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 t^{3} \\\\ t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component.\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=-2.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 t^{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both contributions and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(-2\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}2 t^{3} \\\\ t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- 2 t - 2\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}6 t^{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $\\mathbf h(t)=u(t)\\mathbf f(t)$. For each component, the two terms are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h_{1}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(- 4 t^{3}\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(6 t^{2} \\left(- 2 t - 2\\right)\\big)\\\\&=- 16 t^{3} - 12 t^{2}\\\\h_{2}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(- 2 t\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- 2 t - 2\\big)\\\\&=- 4 t - 2\\\\h_{3}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(2\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(0\\big)\\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}- 16 t^{3} - 12 t^{2} \\\\ - 4 t - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723369 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=t - 1,\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t^{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component.\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=1.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 4 t \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both contributions and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t^{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(t - 1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}- 4 t \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $\\mathbf h(t)=u(t)\\mathbf f(t)$. For each component, the two terms are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h_{1}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(- 2 t^{2}\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- 4 t \\left(t - 1\\right)\\big)\\\\&=- 6 t^{2} + 4 t\\\\h_{2}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(2\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(0\\big)\\\\&=2\\\\h_{3}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\big(t\\big)\\\\&\\quad+\\big(t - 1\\big)\\\\&=2 t - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t^{2} + 4 t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 t - 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723394 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x+\\sin y,\\ y+\\cos x\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le1,\\ 0\\le y\\le x^2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Region below y=x squared, above y=0, between x=0 and x=1.](images/35/3572e500529bb0e2e4a21ed94d0f31a1b5edc3406a572d7173225f4fc440a606.png)\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, use\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on an open set containing $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ pointing outward, Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds\n=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x+\\sin y$ and $Q=y+\\cos x$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nThus the divergence is $2$. The supplied bounds describe a type I region. Integrating first with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^{x^2}2\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[2y]_{y=0}^{y=x^2}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 2x^2\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $\\frac{2}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723399 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2+\\sin y,\\ xy+e^x\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=x^3$, $y=0$, and $x=1$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on an open set containing $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ pointing outward, Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds\n=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x^2+\\sin y$ and $Q=xy+e^x$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nThe divergence is $3x$. The curve $y=x^3$ meets $y=0$ at $x=0$. From there to $x=1$, the region lies above $y=0$ and below $y=x^3$:\n\n![Region below y=x cubed, above y=0, and left of x=1.](images/26/26cbf8624da7845c8c2022254893e53b6dc85561f9878dc5989d11eaad035bac.png)\n\nHence its type I description is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le1,\\ 0\\le y\\le x^3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^{x^3}3x\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[3xy]_{y=0}^{y=x^3}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 3x^4\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3x^5}{5}\\right]_0^1=\\frac{3}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $\\frac{3}{5}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723400 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2+\\sin y,\\ xy+e^x\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $x=-2y$, $x=\\sqrt{y}$, and $y=1$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on an open set containing $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ pointing outward, Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds\n=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x^2+\\sin y$ and $Q=xy+e^x$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=x.\n$$\n\nThe divergence is $3x$. Since $\\sqrt{y}\\ge0$ and $-2y\\le0$ for $y\\ge0$, the two curves meet only at $(0,0)$. The top boundary is $y=1$. A horizontal slice runs from $x=-2y$ to $x=\\sqrt{y}$:\n\n![Region between x=-2y on the left and x=square root of y on the right, from y=0 to y=1; a horizontal slice is shown.](images/6e/6e28b8a2920142f7847d2813fc350248b8bbdf4c1fced6d89ba823ce778e483a.png)\n\nThus a type II description avoids splitting the region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le y\\le1,\\ -2y\\le x\\le\\sqrt{y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_{-2y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\,dx\\,dy\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1\\left[\\frac{3x^2}{2}\\right]_{x=-2y}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dy\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\int_0^1(y-4y^2)\\,dy\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left[\\frac{y^2}{2}-\\frac{4y^3}{3}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(\\frac12-\\frac43\\right)=-\\frac54.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $-\\frac54$. Its negative sign means the net flux points into the region. The left side's negative contribution outweighs the right side's positive contribution; the region is not symmetric about the $y$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723404 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}e^{u} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=4 t - 3$. Find $(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer function component by component and the scalar inner function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf f'(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}e^{u} \\\\ - \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u'(t) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the inner input and multiply **every component** by the same scalar derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t) & =\\mathbf f'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4 t - 3} \\\\ - \\sin{\\left(4 t - 3 \\right)} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(4 t - 3 \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot\\left(4\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 e^{4 t - 3} \\\\ - 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t - 3 \\right)} \\\\ 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t - 3 \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProducts and reciprocals of exponential factors simplify by adding or subtracting their exponents."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723407 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{u} \\\\ \\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=e^{3 t}$. Find $(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer function component by component and the scalar inner function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf f'(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 u \\\\ \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{u}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u'(t) & =3 e^{3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the inner input and multiply **every component** by the same scalar derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t) & =\\mathbf f'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 e^{3 t} \\\\ \\frac{e^{- \\frac{3 t}{2}}}{2} \\\\ e^{- 3 t}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot\\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 e^{6 t} \\\\ \\frac{3 e^{\\frac{3 t}{2}}}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProducts and reciprocals of exponential factors simplify by adding or subtracting their exponents. Here $u(t)>0$. Taking the square root of a positive exponential halves its exponent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723409 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ u^{3} \\\\ \\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=e^{- t}$. Find $(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer function component by component and the scalar inner function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf f'(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ 3 u^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u'(t) & =- e^{- t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the inner input and multiply **every component** by the same scalar derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t) & =\\mathbf f'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sin{\\left(e^{- t} \\right)} \\\\ 3 e^{- 2 t} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot\\left(- e^{- t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}e^{- t} \\sin{\\left(e^{- t} \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{- 3 t} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProducts and reciprocals of exponential factors simplify by adding or subtracting their exponents. Here $u(t)>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723416 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} \\\\ u \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=e^{- 4 t}$. Find $(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer function component by component and the scalar inner function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf f'(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 u \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u'(t) & =- 4 e^{- 4 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the inner input and multiply **every component** by the same scalar derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t) & =\\mathbf f'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 e^{- 4 t} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot\\left(- 4 e^{- 4 t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 8 e^{- 8 t} \\\\ - 4 e^{- 4 t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProducts and reciprocals of exponential factors simplify by adding or subtracting their exponents. Here $u(t)>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723430 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ u^{2} \\\\ \\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$. Find $(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t)$. Work on the real domain $t>1$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the outer function component by component and the scalar inner function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf f'(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 u \\\\ \\frac{1}{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u'(t) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the inner input and multiply **every component** by the same scalar derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t) & =\\mathbf f'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{8 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723441 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,1)$, and $(-1,1)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2y+xy^2,\\,-xy^2+y^3\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, the flux form of Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$, and the counterclockwise boundary has the required positive orientation.\n\nThe region $D$ and its boundary $C$ are shown below.\n\n![Rectangle with horizontal range -1 to 2 and vertical range 0 to 1.](images/a4/a418f22532577cce1962726ecf6ed61537b25c2df0c71de395ccdef462d68e55.png)\n\nThe component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2xy+y^2,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-2xy+3y^2.\n$$\n\nAdding, rather than subtracting, cancels the mixed terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=4y^2.\n$$\n\nThe rectangle is $D=\\{(x,y):-1\\leq x\\leq2,\\ 0\\leq y\\leq1\\}$. Integrating with respect to $y$ first gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_0^1 4y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe inner bounds belong to $y$ and the outer bounds belong to $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723444 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $y=x^2$ and $y=2-x^2$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2y+xy,\\,-xy^2+y^2\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, the flux form of Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$, and the counterclockwise boundary has the required positive orientation.\n\nFirst find where the boundaries meet:\n\n$$\nx^2=2-x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=-1\\text{ or }x=1.\n$$\n\nBoth intersection points have $y=1$. Between these $x$-values, the lower boundary is $y=x^2$ and the upper boundary is $y=2-x^2$.\n\n![Region between an upward parabola and a downward parabola, meeting at (-1,1) and (1,1).](images/1e/1ea32c48979cb2b4e5370b599d184b16ab1f37525b886c2ce3161f459c0232d6.png)\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\leq x\\leq1,\\ x^2\\leq y\\leq2-x^2\\}.\n$$\n\nFor the field components,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2xy+y,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-2xy+2y,\n$$\n\nso the divergence is $3y$. Combining this integrand with the bounds in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{x^2}^{2-x^2}3y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing constant bounds $0\\leq y\\leq2$ would instead include points outside the curved region."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723446 :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^2+y^3,\\,2xy+y^2\\rangle\n$$\n\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf n$ is the outward unit normal, and $D$ is the enclosed region. Calculate $f(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, the flux form of Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C\\left(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\right)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$, and the counterclockwise boundary has the required positive orientation.\n\nThe region is shown below.\n\n![The triangular region bounded by the coordinate axes and the line through (1,0) and (0,2).](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nHere $P=x^2+y^3$ and $Q=2xy+y^2$. Differentiate each component with respect to its matching coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2x,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+(2x+2y)=4x+2y.\n$$\n\nThe triangle specifies the integration region; its bounds are not needed to find the integrand."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723460 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} - y + \\frac{e^{x + 2 y}}{2}\\\\y - e^{x + 2 y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} - 2 y \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\\\x + \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x + 2 y \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{y}{2}\\\\\\frac{3 x}{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{x + 2 y} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=- e^{x + 2 y},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- e^{x + 2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(e^{x + 2 y} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 e^{x} e^{2 y} + 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{3}{2},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **II and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723464 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} + y + e^{x + 2 y}\\\\2 x + 2 e^{x + 2 y} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{e^{x + 2 y}}{2}\\\\x + y - e^{x + 2 y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 x y^{3} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\- 3 x^{2} y^{2} + x + y\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 e^{x + 2 y} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=2 e^{x + 2 y} + 2,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 e^{x + 2 y} + 2\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 e^{x + 2 y} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{x + 2 y},\\\\\nQ_x&=- e^{x + 2 y} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- e^{x + 2 y} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(e^{x + 2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 e^{x} e^{2 y} + 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 6 x y^{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 6 x y^{2} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 6 x y^{2} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 6 x y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **I and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723467 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}2 x y^{3} + \\frac{y}{2} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\3 x^{2} y^{2} + x\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{y}{2} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\\frac{x}{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x - e^{x + 2 y}\\\\- x + y^{2} - 2 e^{x + 2 y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=6 x y^{2} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=6 x y^{2} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(6 x y^{2} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(6 x y^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $\\frac{1}{2}$. Do not select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 e^{x + 2 y},\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 e^{x + 2 y} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 e^{x + 2 y} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 e^{x + 2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **II only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723478 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x + 2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - y\\\\- 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x - \\frac{y}{2} - \\frac{e^{2 y}}{2}\\\\- x e^{2 y} + \\frac{x}{2} + y^{2}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2} + y^{2} - 2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - y\\\\\\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- e^{2 y} - \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=- e^{2 y} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- e^{2 y} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- e^{2 y} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 y - 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 y - 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 y + 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,1)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **I and II only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723497 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and let $D$ be the region it encloses. Which of the following vector fields make\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\operatorname{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nfor every such curve?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n**I.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{I}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 y \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**II.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{II}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}y - e^{2 y} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\- 2 x e^{2 y} + y\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\n**III.**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}_{III}(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}- \\frac{y}{2} + 2 e^{x + y} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\\\\\frac{x}{2} + 2 e^{x + y}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nAll the displayed functions have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. For the circulation to equal area for every such region, the scalar curl must be identically $1$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n**I.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **I**.\n\n**II.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=- 2 e^{2 y} + 1,\\\\\nQ_x&=- 2 e^{2 y},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(- 2 e^{2 y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(- 2 e^{2 y} + 1\\right)\\\\\n&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not identically $1$: at $(x,y)=(0,0)$ its value is $-1$. Do not select **II**.\n\n**III.** \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2 e^{x + y} - \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x&=2 e^{x + y} + \\frac{1}{2},\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(2 e^{x + y} + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\left(2 e^{x + y} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scalar curl is identically $1$, so select **III**.\n\nTherefore, select **I and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723505 :question/problem "Given $f(t)=(- t^{3} - 1)\\,i+(3 t^{2} + 3)\\,j+t^{2}\\,k$, evaluate $(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime\\prime}(t)\\cdot f(t)+f^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot f^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- t^{3} - 1)=- 3 t^{2} \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{2} + 3)=6 t \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})=2 t \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =- 3 t^{2}\\,i+6 t\\,j+2 t\\,k \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t^{2})=- 6 t \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(6 t)=6 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t)=2 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}(t) & =- 6 t\\,i+6\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(0)(-1)+(6)(3)+(2)(0) \\\\  & =18 \\\\ P_2 & =(0)(0)+(0)(0)+(0)(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(0) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723509 :question/problem "Find $(f\\cdot g)^{\\prime}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =(t - 2)\\,i- 2 t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(t^{2} + t)\\,i+t\\,j+t^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f\\cdot g)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot g^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t - 2)=1 \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 t)=-2 \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)=1 \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2} + t)=2 t + 1 \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)=1 \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})=3 t^{2} \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t) & =(2 t + 1)\\,i+\\,j+3 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(1)(t^{2} + t)+(-2)(t)+(1)(t^{3}) \\\\  & =t^{3} + t^{2} - t \\\\ P_2 & =(t - 2)(2 t + 1)+(- 2 t)(1)+(t)(3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =3 t^{3} + 2 t^{2} - 5 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =4 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} - 6 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723513 :question/problem "Find $(f\\cdot g)^{\\prime}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =- 3 \\ln{(t )}\\,i- 2 t\\,j+\\frac{2}{t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =- 3 t\\,i+3 \\ln{(t )}\\,j- \\frac{2}{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$ Assume $t>0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f\\cdot g)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot g^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{(t )})=- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 t)=-2 \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)=- \\frac{2}{t^{2}} \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =- \\frac{3}{t}\\,i-2\\,j- \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\,k \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t)=-3 \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\ln{(t )})=\\frac{3}{t} \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{2}{t}\\right)=\\frac{2}{t^{2}} \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t) & =-3\\,i+\\frac{3}{t}\\,j+\\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =\\left(- \\frac{3}{t}\\right)(- 3 t)+(-2)(3 \\ln{(t )})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{2}{t}\\right) \\\\  & =- 6 \\ln{(t )} + 9 + \\frac{4}{t^{3}} \\\\ P_2 & =(- 3 \\ln{(t )})(-3)+(- 2 t)\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =9 \\ln{(t )} - 6 + \\frac{4}{t^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =3 \\ln{(t )} + 3 + \\frac{8}{t^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723518 :question/problem "Given $f(t)=⟨3 t^{3} + 3,\\,2 t,\\,2 t^{2} + 1⟩$, find $(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime\\prime}(t)\\cdot f(t)+f^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot f^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{3} + 3)=9 t^{2} \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t)=2 \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2} + 1)=4 t \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨9 t^{2},\\,2,\\,4 t⟩ \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(9 t^{2})=18 t \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2)=0 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 t)=4 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}(t) & =⟨18 t,\\,0,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(18 t)(3 t^{3} + 3)+(0)(2 t)+(4)(2 t^{2} + 1) \\\\  & =54 t^{4} + 8 t^{2} + 54 t + 4 \\\\ P_2 & =(9 t^{2})(9 t^{2})+(2)(2)+(4 t)(4 t) \\\\  & =81 t^{4} + 16 t^{2} + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =135 t^{4} + 24 t^{2} + 54 t + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
